'''''Stictocardia beraviensis''''' is a species of rapidly growing perennial vining plant in the family [[Convolvulaceae]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Stictocardia beraviensis (Vatke) Hallier fil. |url=https://www.gbif.org/species/171382192 |access-date=2021-10-19 |website=www.gbif.org |publisher=[[GBIF]] |language=en}}</ref><ref name="powo">{{Cite web|url=http://powo.science.kew.org/taxon/urn:lsid:ipni.org:names:271218-1|title=Stictocardia beraviensis (Vatke) Hallier f. &#124; Plants of the World Online &#124; Kew Science|website=Plants of the World Online}}</ref> It is native to West Africa and Madagascar and widely cultivated elsewhere for its soft, cordate leaves and attractive red flowers.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Stictocardia beraviensis - Some Magnetic Island Plants|url=https://www.somemagneticislandplants.com.au/hawaiian-sunset-vine|access-date=2021-10-19|website=www.somemagneticislandplants.com.au|language=en-gb}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=admin|title=Stictocardia beraviensis|url=http://www.liberianfaunaflora.org/liberian-flora/convolvulaceae/1879-stictocardia-beraviensis|access-date=2021-10-19|website=Fauna & Flora Of Liberia|language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Stictocardia beraviensis (Vatke) Hallier f. — The Plant List|url=http://www.theplantlist.org/tpl1.1/record/tro-8501838|access-date=2021-10-19|website=www.theplantlist.org}}</ref> It is commonly called '''Hawaiian bell vine''' although this is a misnomer as it is in reality not native to Hawaii.

